Title: PDFium: fall back to a loadable font, and survive GetDC failing
Origin: electron-legacy (this repo), after Supermium M156
       (https://github.com/win32ss/supermium)

Two changes to PDFium's font handling on Windows, both taken from Supermium's
pdfium font fix and both for the benefit of the PDF viewer Electron embeds.

1. core/fxge/cfx_font.cpp. CFX_Font::LoadSubstFace asks the font mapper for a
   face to stand in for a font the document names but does not embed. The
   mapper can return null -- it picked a system font and then could not load
   it, which on older Windows happens with raster fonts that have no outline
   data to give -- and every character set in that font is then left
   undrawn. When that happens two fonts that can be loaded are tried in turn:
   Arial Unicode MS, present where Office is, and Tahoma, present everywhere.

   Two things differ from the Supermium original. Each retry starts from a
   fresh CFX_SubstFont rather than reusing the one a failed lookup has
   already written to. And the font's data is recorded for whichever attempt
   succeeds: as written there, the Tahoma branch set the face and not
   font_data_, and the Skia device will not draw text from a font whose data
   span is empty (fx_skia_device.cpp returns false for it), so the last
   fallback was the one that did least.

   Windows only. The font names mean nothing elsewhere.

2. xfa/fgas/font/cfgas_fontmgr.cpp. EnumGdiFonts did CHECK(hdc) on the
   result of GetDC(nullptr), turning a failed call into a crash. It returns
   the empty list instead, which its callers already handle. XFA is enabled
   in Windows builds (pdf_enable_xfa_override), so this code is in the
   binary.

Applies unchanged in substance to PDFium as of Chromium 152; neither file is
touched by anything else in the series.

diff --git a/third_party/pdfium/core/fxge/cfx_font.cpp b/third_party/pdfium/core/fxge/cfx_font.cpp
--- a/third_party/pdfium/core/fxge/cfx_font.cpp
+++ b/third_party/pdfium/core/fxge/cfx_font.cpp
@@ -213,9 +213,32 @@
   vertical_ = bVertical;
   object_tag_ = 0;
   subst_font_ = std::make_unique<CFX_SubstFont>();
-  face_ = CFX_GEModule::Get()->GetFontMgr()->GetBuiltinMapper()->FindSubstFace(
-      face_name, bTrueType, flags, weight, italic_angle, code_page,
-      subst_font_.get());
+  CFX_FontMapper* mapper = CFX_GEModule::Get()->GetFontMgr()->GetBuiltinMapper();
+  face_ = mapper->FindSubstFace(face_name, bTrueType, flags, weight,
+                                italic_angle, code_page, subst_font_.get());
+#if BUILDFLAG(IS_WIN)
+  // The mapper can come back with nothing: it found a system font to stand in
+  // for `face_name` and then could not load it. On older Windows that is not
+  // rare -- a raster font such as MS Sans Serif has no outline data to hand
+  // over -- and text set in it is then simply not drawn. Two fonts that can
+  // be loaded, and cover a great deal between them, are tried before giving
+  // up: Arial Unicode MS where Office has installed it, and Tahoma, which
+  // every Windows since 2000 has.
+  //
+  // After Supermium, which adds the same two. Unlike that patch, each attempt
+  // starts from a fresh CFX_SubstFont, since a failed lookup leaves the old
+  // one part filled in, and the font data is recorded for whichever attempt
+  // succeeds -- there the last fallback returned a face without its data,
+  // which the Skia device then refuses to draw text with.
+  for (const char* fallback : {"Arial Unicode MS", "Tahoma"}) {
+    if (face_) {
+      break;
+    }
+    subst_font_ = std::make_unique<CFX_SubstFont>();
+    face_ = mapper->FindSubstFace(fallback, bTrueType, flags, weight,
+                                  italic_angle, code_page, subst_font_.get());
+  }
+#endif  // BUILDFLAG(IS_WIN)
   if (face_) {
     font_data_ = face_->GetData();
   }
diff --git a/third_party/pdfium/xfa/fgas/font/cfgas_fontmgr.cpp b/third_party/pdfium/xfa/fgas/font/cfgas_fontmgr.cpp
--- a/third_party/pdfium/xfa/fgas/font/cfgas_fontmgr.cpp
+++ b/third_party/pdfium/xfa/fgas/font/cfgas_fontmgr.cpp
@@ -216,7 +216,12 @@
     dest_face_name_with_null[src_face_name.size() + 1] = 0;
   }
   HDC hdc = ::GetDC(nullptr);
-  CHECK(hdc);
+  // No device context is no fonts, not a reason to end the process: GetDC can
+  // fail where the desktop heap is exhausted, and an empty list is something
+  // every caller already handles. From Supermium.
+  if (!hdc) {
+    return fonts;
+  }
   EnumFontFamiliesExW(hdc, (LPLOGFONTW)&lfFind, (FONTENUMPROCW)GdiFontEnumProc,
                       (LPARAM)&fonts, 0);
   ::ReleaseDC(nullptr, hdc);
